---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import HeartSim from '../../simulators/heart/HeartSim.astro';
import { heartQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/heart';

const toc = [
  { id: 'structure', label: 'Structure of the heart' },
  { id: 'pathway', label: 'The pathway of blood' },
  { id: 'double', label: 'Double circulation' },
  { id: 'cycle', label: 'The cardiac cycle' },
  { id: 'output', label: 'Heart rate and cardiac output' },
  { id: 'vessels', label: 'Arteries, veins and capillaries' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Betts, J. G., et al. (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e, Section 19.1: Heart Anatomy. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/19-1-heart-anatomy' },
  { text: 'Betts, J. G., et al. (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e, Section 19.3: Cardiac Cycle. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/19-3-cardiac-cycle' },
  { text: 'Betts, J. G., et al. (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e, Section 19.4: Cardiac Physiology. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/19-4-cardiac-physiology' },
  { text: 'Hall, J. E., & Hall, M. E. (2020). Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology (14th ed.). Elsevier.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="anatomy-physiology"
  topic="heart-circulation"
  description="Interactive heart simulator: watch the four chambers and valves through the cardiac cycle, follow a blood cell through the lungs and body, and change heart rate and stroke volume."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={heartQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Your heart beats about 100,000 times a day, pushing blood through two loops: one to the lungs to collect oxygen,
      and one to the rest of the body to deliver it. Watch the chambers squeeze, see the valves open and shut, and follow a
      single blood cell all the way round.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <HeartSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Watch the <strong>phase bar</strong> at the top right and the panel text: atria contract, then ventricles, then the heart relaxes.</li>
      <li>Turn on <strong>Slow motion</strong> to see the <strong>valves</strong> (white when open, yellow when shut) and hear where “lub” and “dub” come from.</li>
      <li>Keep <strong>Follow one blood cell</strong> on and read the <strong>Tracked cell is in</strong> readout to trace the full route.</li>
      <li>Use <strong>Asleep</strong>, <strong>Resting</strong> and <strong>Running</strong>, or the sliders, to change heart rate and stroke volume and watch the cardiac output change.</li>
      <li><strong>Click any chamber, valve or vessel</strong> to learn what it does.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="structure">Structure of the heart</h2>
  <p>
    The heart is a muscular pump about the size of a fist, sitting between the lungs. It has four chambers: two
    <strong>atria</strong> on top that receive blood, and two <strong>ventricles</strong> below that pump it out. A wall
    called the <strong>septum</strong> separates the right side (deoxygenated blood) from the left side (oxygenated blood).
    Diagrams show the heart as if you were facing the person, so the right side of the heart appears on the left of the
    picture.
  </p>
  <p>
    Four <strong>valves</strong> keep blood moving one way. The <strong>tricuspid</strong> and <strong>mitral</strong>
    (bicuspid) valves sit between each atrium and ventricle. The <strong>pulmonary</strong> and <strong>aortic</strong>
    valves sit where blood leaves the ventricles.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pathway">The pathway of blood</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Deoxygenated blood from the body returns through the <strong>venae cavae</strong> to the <strong>right atrium</strong>.</li>
    <li>It passes through the tricuspid valve into the <strong>right ventricle</strong>.</li>
    <li>The right ventricle pumps it through the pulmonary valve into the <strong>pulmonary artery</strong> and on to the lungs.</li>
    <li>In the lung capillaries it picks up oxygen and gives up carbon dioxide.</li>
    <li>Oxygenated blood returns through the <strong>pulmonary veins</strong> to the <strong>left atrium</strong>.</li>
    <li>It passes through the mitral valve into the <strong>left ventricle</strong>.</li>
    <li>The left ventricle pumps it through the aortic valve into the <strong>aorta</strong>, which branches to every organ.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="double">Double circulation</h2>
  <p>
    Mammals and birds have a <strong>double circulation</strong>: blood passes through the heart twice on each full trip.
    The <strong>pulmonary circuit</strong> carries blood between the heart and lungs at low pressure, which protects the
    delicate lung capillaries. The <strong>systemic circuit</strong> carries blood to the rest of the body at high pressure,
    so it can reach the brain, the feet and everywhere between. That is why the left ventricle has a much thicker muscular
    wall than the right.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cycle">The cardiac cycle</h2>
  <p>
    Each heartbeat follows the same sequence. In <strong>atrial systole</strong> the atria contract, topping up the
    ventricles. In <strong>ventricular systole</strong> the ventricles contract: the pressure rise slams the tricuspid and
    mitral valves shut (the first heart sound, “lub”) and forces the pulmonary and aortic valves open. In
    <strong>diastole</strong> the muscle relaxes, the pulmonary and aortic valves shut (the second sound, “dub”), and blood
    flows in from the veins to refill the chambers. The beat is started by the heart’s own pacemaker, the
    <strong>sinoatrial node</strong>, in the wall of the right atrium.
  </p>

  <h2 id="output">Heart rate and cardiac output</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cardiac output</strong> is the volume of blood each ventricle pumps per minute:
  </p>
  <p><code class="formula">cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume</code></p>
  <p>
    At rest, 70 beats per minute × 70 mL ≈ 4.9 L per minute: roughly all of your blood every minute. During hard exercise,
    adrenaline and nerve signals raise both the rate and the strength of each beat, and output can reach 15–20 L per
    minute in a fit adult, or more in trained athletes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="vessels">Arteries, veins and capillaries</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Arteries</strong> carry blood away from the heart. They have thick, elastic, muscular walls to cope with high pressure.</li>
    <li><strong>Veins</strong> carry blood back to the heart at low pressure. They have thinner walls and valves to stop backflow.</li>
    <li><strong>Capillaries</strong> are only one cell thick, so oxygen, nutrients and waste can pass between blood and tissues.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Atrium / ventricle</dt>
    <dd>Upper receiving chamber / lower pumping chamber of the heart.</dd>
    <dt>Systole / diastole</dt>
    <dd>Contraction / relaxation of the heart muscle.</dd>
    <dt>Stroke volume</dt>
    <dd>Volume of blood pumped by one ventricle in one beat.</dd>
    <dt>Septum</dt>
    <dd>Muscular wall dividing the left and right sides of the heart.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Arteries always carry oxygenated blood.”</strong> The pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood. Arteries are defined by direction: away from the heart.</li>
    <li><strong>“Deoxygenated blood is blue.”</strong> It is dark red. Blue is just a diagram convention.</li>
    <li><strong>“The two sides of the heart beat one after the other.”</strong> Left and right contract together; it is the atria and ventricles that take turns.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <p>
      <strong>Educational model only.</strong> This simulator is a simplified diagram, not a medical tool. It cannot
      diagnose or assess any heart condition, and the values shown are typical textbook figures. If you have concerns about
      your heart or health, speak to a qualified healthcare professional. See our <a href="/disclaimer/">educational and
      medical disclaimer</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The right side of the heart pumps blood to the lungs; the left side pumps it to the body.</li>
    <li>Valves keep blood flowing one way and make the “lub-dub” sounds as they shut.</li>
    <li>Each beat is atrial systole, ventricular systole, then diastole.</li>
    <li>Cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: the diagram is schematic, not to scale, and the vessels are rearranged so the two circuits are easy to follow. One trip round the loop takes about 8 beats here; in a resting person it takes about a minute.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
